{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law criminal-conviction and threat grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant who is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is civilly found liable for assault, threats, theft or illegal drug activity on the premises, or human trafficking; a three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Tenancies covered by the Eviction Law; also a tenant who lets a person he knows was convicted of or pleaded guilty to such offenses reside at the premises continuously or occasionally.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"Under the civil-liability ground, does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family eviction causes and notice","requirement":"For landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, a tenant may be removed only by court order and only for the listed causes; a three-month or one-month notice to quit applies, with a three-day written Notice to Quit for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating causes.","key_value":"3-day written Notice to Quit","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied (owner-occupied) two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three-day written Notice to Quit is required for any of the causes described as disorderly, destructive or violative of written rules or lease provisions (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law penalties for personal-occupancy and removal-from-residential-use evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicted to personally occupy a unit must occupy it at least six months or owe three times damages plus fees; a landlord who permanently retired a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' occupancy; 5-year bar; $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtained a tenant's departure on notice of personal occupancy or of permanent boarding-up, demolition or retirement from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation of the five-year restriction.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal for the tenant's exercise of listed civil rights; applies to all rental dwellings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure tenant protections and 90-day notice","requirement":"Before evicting a tenant because of foreclosure, a landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit when the buyer wants to personally occupy; a lease running beyond 90 days bars eviction until it expires, and new owners must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant remaining in a foreclosed property; 90-day notice required when the buyer will personally occupy.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"The 90-day notice may be given 90 days before the lease expires. Month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirements.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt state or local law giving tenants longer periods or additional protections.","relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Eligible senior, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled-veteran tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within the limit.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income limit 3× county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged and 60%-disabled veterans, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant age, disability or veteran status and one year of occupancy before conversion recording.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"Extends protected-tenancy eviction protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981; Hudson County is the only qualified county.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties (at present only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting the eviction action.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception applies.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"units","role":"covered","op":"at_least","n":2}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathroo ms is a rooming or boarding house if it does not meet one (1) of the exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act (N.J.S.A. 55:13B-3)."]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","n_rules":8,"note":null}

